5. How to Test in Postman

Step 1: Get an Access Token

1. Open Postman.


2. Send a POST request to:

https://yourtenant.b2clogin.com/yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com/B2C_1_your_policy/oauth2/v2.0/token


3. Body (x-www-form-urlencoded):

grant_type: password
client_id: your-client-id
client_secret: your-client-secret
scope: https://yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com/your-api-scope/user_impersonation offline_access
username: your-user@example.com
password: your-password


4. Copy the access_token and refresh_token.




---

Step 2: Validate the Token

1. Open Postman.


2. Send a GET request to:

https://your-api.com/api/auth/validate-token


3. Headers:

Authorization: Bearer your-access-token


4. If the token is valid, you will receive:

{
  "message": "Token Valid",
  "claims": [
    { "Type": "email", "Value": "user@example.com" }
  ]
}


5. If the token is expired, you will receive:

{
  "message": "Token Expired",
  "refreshRequired": true
}




---

Step 3: Refresh the Token

1. Send a POST request to:

https://your-api.com/api/auth/refresh-token


2. Body (JSON):

{
  "refreshToken": "your-refresh-token"
}


3. If successful, you will receive a new access token:

{
  "access_token": "new-access-token"
}


4. Use this new token for further API calls.




